window.docContents[139].resolve({"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Analytic-Model-Methods--Power-series-and-analytic-model-methods":{"contents":"Power-series methods build a solution from its Taylor coefficients, and the\nfirst requirement is that the equation determine the normal derivative of\nthe unknown on the initial surface. For a first-order scalar equation\n, with  standing for the value of  and  for its\nderivative normal to the flat surface , that determination is an\nimplicit-function problem in the single variable . Once a system is in\nnormal form, the coupled analytic evolution theorem solves it.\n\nAn equation  is noncharacteristic in  at\n whenThis is the scalar implicit-function condition for the flat surface\n.\n\nNonvanishing of  at a point is precisely the invertibility\nhypothesis of the implicit-function theorem for the map\n, once the initial datum  is substituted\nfor . The step determines only the first normal derivative. Cauchy–Kovalevskaya goes further,\nsolving a recurrence for all higher normal derivatives and summing the\nresulting power series.\n\nAssume , the derivative in  is nonzero\nthere, and the composite  has positive\nregularity order  at . Then an open neighborhood of\n carries a graph  withthroughout that neighborhood, , and the same regularity order\nat . The theorem covers finite, smooth, and analytic orders. Choosing\nMathlib's analytic order  gives the analytic initial graph.\n\nThe scalar nonzero derivative gives the invertible partial derivative\nrequired by Mathlib's implicit-function theorem. Apply it to the composite\nat order . Its graph preserves the level ,\nwhich is zero by the base equation, and passes through the prescribed point.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Representing Solutions\u0009Explicit Representations and Transform Methods\u0009Analytic Model Methods","header":"9.2.1.8.1. Power-series and analytic model methods","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Analytic-Model-Methods--Power-series-and-analytic-model-methods"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#textbook-coverage":{"contents":"Cards with source annotations record a document and a cited location. The\ntables group those cards by the table of contents of Evans's Partial\nDifferential Equations (second edition), chapter by chapter, followed by\nbriefer per-location tables for the other documents. Evans references use the\nAMS second edition (2010). The map's section starts follow its table of\ncontents; page references for selected passages are recorded in\nBlueprint/Sources/Evans.md.\n\nA CAS chip marks a card with a declaration that contains a production call\nsite of the calculator in the computation ledger. It records use of the\ncalculator inside a declaration, without measuring how much of the proof that\ncalculation supplies.\n\nThe counts are counts of citing cards. A card may specialize or support the\nresult it cites, and its statement gives the precise hypotheses and\nconclusion. No mapped card means that no card cites that section. Problem\nand reference sections are listed for completeness.\n\nEach card is placed by the first page it cites, so a card citing pages 47 to\n51 counts once, in the section containing page 47. Section boundaries come\nfrom the verified contents recorded in the generator. We regenerate the tables\nfrom the card citations whenever we build the Blueprint. Each card name links\nto its statement.\n\n\n\nThe remaining source documents are cited far less densely, so for them the map\nlists the cited locations as the cards give them: a chapter, a page range, a\nsection, or an equation number.\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation","header":"10.11. Textbook Coverage","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#textbook-coverage"}});